THIRD-PARTY DATASHEET TEXT INSIDE THE ARCHIVE
  The archive records an agent navigating technical datasheets published by
  third parties, and it quotes those documents at length. The quoted text lives
  in `archive/*.jsonl` (fields `tool_output_summary` and `agent_reasoning`) and
  also in `archive/*.json` (fields `source_text` and `submitted_source_text`).

    - Si115x                Silicon Laboratories Inc.
    - ACS70331, A4988       Allegro MicroSystems, LLC
    - DPS310                Infineon Technologies AG (ours, but see below)

  We would rather state the extent plainly than characterise it favourably.
  Measuring verbatim six-word sequences shared between each document's text
  layer and the whole archive, two ways -- by position (what share of the
  document's running text appears verbatim somewhere) and by distinct sequence:

                    by position      distinct sequences
    Si115x              ~44%               ~35%
    DPS310              ~53%               ~27%
    ACS70331            ~26%               ~25%
    A4988                ~3%                ~3%

  Individual `tool_output_summary` records are capped at about 1,500
  characters; `agent_reasoning` is not capped. The longest contiguous verbatim
  run in a single record is roughly 260 words of prose, and one 596-word run
  that is a table of contents. Some individual pages are covered far more
  heavily than the document average, and complete parametric tables -- for
  example the Si115x recommended-operating-conditions table -- appear in full,
  with their row and column structure intact.

  Partial reconstruction is possible, and we would rather say so than imply
  otherwise. Tool outputs carry explicit page attribution (`Page 9:`,
  `=== Page 1 of 41 ===`), so fragments can be grouped by the page they came
  from; for the most heavily covered documents a number of individual pages can
  be recovered at high fidelity. What is NOT present is any figure, diagram or
  image; any page geometry or layout coordinates; and any complete document.

